# Multi-modal anti-counterfeiting and encryption enabled through silicon-based materials featuring pH-responsive fluorescence and room-temperature phosphorescence

Jinhua Wang§, Bin Song§, Jiali Tang, Guyue Hu, Jingyang Wang, Mingyue Cui, and Yao He $\boxed{\boxtimes}$
Laboratory of Nanoscale Biochemical Analysis, Institute of Functional Nano and Soft Materials (FUNSOM), and Collaborative Innovation Center of Suzhou Nano Science and Technology, Soochow University, Suzhou 215123, China   
§ Jinhua Wang and Bin Song contributed equally to this work.
$\circledcirc$ Tsinghua University Press and Springer-Verlag GmbH Germany, part of Springer Nature 2020   
Received: 22 December 2019 / Revised: 28 March 2020 / Accepted: 28 March 2020
## ABSTRACT
Optical silicon (Si)-based materials are highly attractive due to their widespread applications ranging from electronics to biomedicine. It is worth noting that while extensive efforts have been devoted to developing fluorescent Si-based structures, there currently exist no examples of Si-based materials featuring phosphorescence emission, severely limiting Si-based wide-ranging optical applications. To address this critical issue, we herein introduce a kind of Si-based material, in which metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are in-situ growing on the surface of Si nanoparticles (SiNPs) assisted by microwave irradiation. Of particular significance, the resultant materials, i.e., MOFs-encapsulated SiNPs (MOFs $@$ SiNPs) could exhibit pH-responsive fluorescence, whose maximum emission wavelength is red-shifted from 442 to 592 nm when the pH increases from 2 to 13. More importantly, distinct roomtemperature phosphorescence (maximum emission wavelength: $505~\mathsf{n m}$ ) could be observed in this system, with long lifetime of 215 ms. Taking advantages of above-mentioned unique optical properties, the MOFs $@$ SiNPs are further employed as high-quality anti-counterfeiting inks for advanced encryption. In comparison to conventional fluorescence anti-counterfeiting techniques (static fluorescence outputs are generally used, thus being easily duplicated and leading to counterfeiting risk), pH-responsive fluorescence and room-temperature phosphorescence of the resultant MOFs $@$ SiNPs-based ink could offer advanced multi-modal security, which is therefore capable of realizing higher-level information security against counterfeiting.
## KEYWORDS
silicon nanohybrids, pH-responsive fluorescence, room-temperature phosphorescence, multi-modal anti-counterfeiting
## Introduction
Optical silicon (Si)-based materials have drawn extensive attentions since the last century. With the significant advancement of nanotechnology, fluorescent zero-dimensional Si nanoparticles (SiNPs) have been successfully prepared since they could produce visible fluorescence when their sizes reach nanoscale level (generally smaller than $5\mathrm{nm}$ ). In recent years, elegant effort has been devoted for the fabrication of high-quality SiNPs featuring strong fluorescence and robust photostability [1–6]. Moreover, small-sized (diameter $<10~\mathrm{{nm}}$ ) SiNPs have recently entered into the first clinic trial due to their excellent biodegradability and negligible toxicity [7, 8]. It is worth pointing out that, while tremendous progresses have been achieved for the production of fluorescent Si-based materials, there currently exist scanty examples of Si-based materials simultaneously featuring fluorescence and phosphorescence properties, which is adverse to promoting Si-based widespread optical applications.
On the other hand, as a global problem, counterfeiting causes severe economic and security impacts on the government, industry, and consumers [9, 10]. Therefore, the development of anti-counterfeiting system with reliable security has triggered extensive research. Over the last decade, a number of anti-counterfeiting technologies have been developed, such as plasmonic security labels [11–13], magnetic response [14, 15], holograms [12] and optical anti-counterfeiting [16–21]. Among them, optical anti-counterfeiting has received increasing attention. Up to now, a variety of luminescent materials, including organic dyes, lanthanide-doped luminescent nanomaterials, quantum dots (QDs), have been used in the fields of data storage and information security [18, 22–25]. Of particular note, most of the luminescent materials employed in anti-counterfeiting generally produce static fluorescence outputs, which are easily duplicated, thus leading to potential counterfeiting risks [26–29]. Therefore, there currently exists high demand to develop novel optical materials, which could offer multimodal optical signals, ready for higher-level information security against counterfeiting.
In this work, we assemble the SiNPs with metal–organic frameworks (MOFs, served as hosts) under microwave irradiation, producing the MOFs-encapsulated SiNPs $(\mathrm{MOFs@SiNPs})$ . Significantly, the resultant MOFs@SiNPs display pH-dependent fluorescence, whose maximum emission wavelength increases from $\sim442\:\mathrm{nm}$ $\mathrm{\dot{\pH}}$ value: 2) to $\sim592\mathrm{nm}$ ( $\mathrm{\dot{\pH}}$ value: 13). Besides, the $\mathsf{M O F s@S i N P s}$ exhibit strong phosphorescence (maximum emission wavelength: $\sim505\mathrm{nm}$ ) under room temperature with long lifetime (more than $5\ s$ to naked eye). Taking advantages of the unique optical features, the $\mathsf{M O F s@S i N P s}$ are further exploited as high-performance optical inks, superbly suitable for the multi-modal encryption and fingerprint anti-counterfeiting.
## 2 Results and discussion
## 2.1 Synthesis of MOFs@SiNPs
Preparation of the MOFs@SiNPs and their luminescent properties are schematically illustrated in Fig. 1. Specifically, the SiNPs can be readily encapsulated by metal−organic frameworks in the self-assembly process through microwave reaction. In our system, there is affinity between SiNPs surfaces and carboxylate groups of organic linker molecules, facilitating the adsorption of the terephthalic acid on the SiNPs surface. Therefore, the zinc carboxylate secondary building units (SBUs) can be formed on the surface of SiNPs (Step 1) [30, 31]. The paddle-wheel dinuclear zinc carboxylate SBUs are bridged by the terephthalic acid (TPA) ligands to form two-dimensional layers. These layers are stacked in an offset fashion, which would produce the three-dimensional frameworks embedded with SiNPs, therefore leading to the formation of the hybrid MOFs $@$ SiNPs (Step 2) [32–35]. Of particular significance, under ultraviolet (UV) irradiation, the resultant $\mathsf{M O F s@S i N P s}$ exhibit blue fluorescence emission at $442~\mathrm{nm}$ (arising from the encapsulated SiNPs). Moreover, the $\mathsf{M O F s@S i N P s}$ show green phosphorescence emission (maximum emission wavelength: $\sim505\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) at room temperature after turning off the UV lamp, which can be attributed to the metal-ligand charge transfer in metal-organic frameworks [35]. In addition, due to the structural transformation of the $\mathsf{M O F s@S i N P s}$ in alkaline condition $\begin{array}{r}{\left(\mathrm{pH}=13\right)}\end{array}$ ), the fluorescence emission can be tuned from blue to orange (with emission wavelength of $592{\mathrm{nm}}$ ).
## 2.2 Characterizations of MOFs@SiNPs
The optical properties of the $\mathsf{M O F s@S i N P s}$ are characterized (Fig. 3, Figs. S7 and S8 in the ESM). The obtained MOFs@SiNPs display blue fluorescence (quantum yield (QY): $\sim17\%$ , Fig. S9 in the ESM) under UV irradiation $(365\mathrm{nm})$ ), and interestingly, green phosphorescence can be observed by the naked eye after turning off the UV light. In this system, the zinc ions contribute to the frontier orbital distribution, which is able to promote spin-orbit coupling. On the other hand, the rigid framework may result in restriction of the molecular vibrations and nonradiative loss, which increases singlet-to-triplet intersystem crossing (ISC) and thus leads to the production of green phosphorescence [42–44]. Moreover, the phosphorescence duration could be recognized for approximately $5s$ by a digital camera (Fig. 3(a)). When dispersed in $\mathrm{\DeltaNaOH}$ solution $\mathrm{\DeltapH=}$ 13), the fluorescence emission of the $\mathsf{M O F s@S i N P s}$ shows a remarkable transformation from a blue to orange colour, while the phosphorescence disappears (Fig. 3(b)). The fluorescence spectrum of $\mathsf{M O F s@S i N P s}$ presents the maximum emission peak at $442\mathrm{nm}$ produced by the encapsulated SiNPs (Fig. 3(c)), suggesting the formation of host−guest system between MOFs and SiNPs. As the $\mathrm{\pH}$ value increases from 2 to 13, the fluorescence emission shows a red shift from 442 to $592\mathrm{nm}$ with excitation wavelength at $365\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figs. S10(a) and S10(b) in the ESM). For the $\mathsf{M O F s@S i N P s}$ , the structural transformation of the Zn-based SBUs occurs due to the hydrolysis of the coordinated N,N-Dimethylformamide (DMF) molecules in the MOFs@SiNPs channels [32, 45, 46], which would induce the reduced band gap, therefore leading to the emission wavelength red-shift [35, 47]. The XRD results further show that the MOFs@SiNPs present an obvious structural change after treatment with $\mathrm{pH}12$ and $\mathrm{pH}13$ basic solution, which are in agreement with TEM characterizations (Figs. S10(c) and S11 in the ESM). In addition to the fluorescence emission under UV excitation, the $\mathsf{M O F s@S i N P s}$ composites exhibit distinct green phosphorescence at about $505\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 3(d)). As shown in Fig. 3(e), phosphorescence decay curves of the MOFs and MOFs@SiNPs can be fitted with a single exponential function. According to the following equation: $I(t)\ =\ A\mathrm{e}^{\wedge}(-1/\tau),$ phosphorescence lifetimes of the MOFs and $\mathsf{M O F s@S i N P s}$ are $226~\mathrm{ms}$ (half-life: $174.10~\mathrm{ms},$ ) and $215~\mathrm{ms}$ (half-life: $171.38~\mathrm{ms},$ ), respectively. For deeper understanding the room-temperature phosphorescence property of the $\begin{array}{r}{\operatorname{MOFs@SiNPs}}\end{array}$ , the low temperature (77 K) fluorescence and phosphorescence spectra of the $\mathsf{M O F s@S i N P s}$ are measured (Fig. S12 in the ESM). Accordingly, the energy gap between the lowest triplet state $(T_{1})$ and the lowest singlet state (S1) is calculated to be $0.33\mathrm{eV}$ 1 $T_{1}=2.53\ \mathrm{eV}_{:}$ , $\ensuremath{\mathrm{S}}_{1}=2.86~\ensuremath{\mathrm{eV}}.$ ), and such a small value is favourable for an effective ISC process. Furthermore, we investigate the photostability of the $\mathsf{M O F s@S i N P s}$ . As shown in Fig. S13 in the ESM, the MOFs@SiNPs maintain stable fluorescence (preserving $\sim84\%$ of the initial intensity) and phosphorescence (preserving $\sim73\%$ of the initial intensity) under continuous UV exposure for $200\ \mathrm{min}$ . The assays of cellular viability (Henrietta Lacks (HeLa) cells and African green monkey kidney cells (COS-7) treated by the $\mathsf{M O F s@S i N P s}$ preserve over $85\%$ at different concentrations and incubation time (12 and $24\mathrm{h})$ ) demonstrate the negligible cytotoxicity of the resultant $\mathsf{M O F s@S i N P s}$ (Fig. S14 in the ESM). Such attractive optical properties and favorable biocompatibility of Si enable the prepared $\mathsf{M O F s@S i N P s}$ an advanced security ink for multi-responsive anti-counterfeiting applications, as demonstrated in the following experiments.
## 2.3 Multi-modal anti-counterfeiting
We further exploit the MOFs@SiNPs as novel high-performance luminescent inks for multi-responsive optical patterns (Video S1 and S2, in the ESM). As shown in Fig. 4(a), numeric codes of $\mathfrak{e}_{92451}{}^{\mathfrak{n}}$ are handwritten with the MOFs@SiNPs ink on commercial A4 paper. After complete drying in ambient conditions, the characters are nearly invisible under daylight. When exposed under UV irradiation, no numbers can be recognized due to the strong fluorescence background of the paper. In sharp contrast, the correct number can be obtained when the UV lamp is turned off. This result can be attributed to the long phosphorescence lifetime of the $\mathsf{M O F s@S i N P s}$ under room temperature. As a proof-of-concept application, the feasibility of $\mathsf{M O F s@S i N P s}$ as a kind of superior security ink for numbers encryption is further investigated. As illustrated in Fig. 4(b), on commercial black paper, the desired different parts of numbers are drawn using the corresponding materials and a white pattern of $^{\alpha}821^{\mathfrak{n}}$ is visible under daylight. In addition, wrong information (blue numbers of $^{\alpha}34^{\prime\prime}$ and orange numbers of $^{\alpha}12^{\prime\prime}$ ) is observed upon UV irradiation. In marked contrast, the correct information (number $^{\alpha}{31}^{\mathfrak{n}}$ ) can be obtained by switching off UV irradiation.
Fingerprints featuring the unique ridge skin patterns hold a wealth of information, which have been applied extensively in identity recognition. In our daily life, fingerprint patterns are often applied in documents, diplomas and certificates to make them hard to duplicate and easy to authenticate. Therefore, we further study the feasibility of the MOFs $@$ SiNPs-based ink for fingerprints recognition application. In our experiment, fingers stained with the $\mathsf{M O F s@S i N P s}$ ink are pressed on commercial black paper, and three-level details of the fingerprint features could be clearly indicated through observing fluorescence and phosphorescence signals of the MOFs@SiNPs. Specifically, three basic fingerprint patterns (level 1) include whorl (Fig. 5(a)), loop (Fig. 5(b)) and double loop whorls (Fig. S15 in the ESM). Both fluorescent and phosphorescent images display wellresolved ridge flow and pattern structure of the fingerprints. In addition, a number of magnified images of these fingerprints are shown in Fig. 5(c), which clearly illustrate details of the fingerprints (termination (level 2), bridge (level 2), short ridge (level 2), and creases (level 3)) [48, 49]. These detailed characteristics will be useful for discerning personal identifying features and providing prima facie evidence in the identification of partial or damaged fingerprints. Moreover, the precise signal-to-noise ratios of phosphorescence and fluorescence imaging are calculated to be 233.64 and 34, respectively, indicating that the MOFs@SiNPs can effectively increase the imaging sensitivity and signal-to-noise ratio. For information protection and other anti-counterfeiting applications, the fingerprints need to be highly resistant to atmosphere and can be stored for long time. Therefore, we further demonstrate the stability of these MOFs@SiNPs-based fingerprints exposed to ambient conditions without any specific protection. As shown in Fig. S16 in the ESM, after 30 days of exposure in the atmosphere, these fingerprints maintain stable and bright fluorescence when irradiated by $365~\mathrm{nm}$ irradiation. When the UV lamp is turned off, the fingerprints still can be easily recognized. The above results clearly demonstrate that the prepared MOFs@SiNPs possess excellent stability.
## 3 Conclusions
In summary, a novel kind of luminescent material, i.e., MOFs@SiNPs, has been prepared through a facile microwaveassisted method. Significantly, the $\mathsf{M O F s@S i N P s}$ display pHresponsive fluorescence emission (maximum emission wavelength from 442 to $592~\mathrm{nm}$ , corresponding to $\mathrm{\pH}$ value from 2 to 13), as well as strong room-temperature phosphorescence (maximum emission at $\sim505\mathrm{nm}$ ) with long lifetime of $215~\mathrm{ms}$ . Further studies demonstrate that the $\mathsf{M O F s@S i N P s}$ could be utilized as high-efficacy security inks for multi-modal anticounterfeiting in number security and fingerprint fields, showing high promise in the field of data storage, information protection and advanced encryption. We expect that this study may offer exciting opportunities for designing new kinds of fluorescent and phosphorescent Si-based materials, facilitating the promotion of Si-based widespread optical applications. Moreover, our results would provide powerful tools for multimodal advanced anti-counterfeiting and encryption with higher-level information security.
## 4 Experimental section
## 4.1 Synthesis of SiNPs
Fluorescent SiNPs were readily synthesized through microwaveassisted method based on the previous report [50]. In details, trisodium citrate dihydrate $\left(0.075\ \mathrm{g}\right)$ was dissolved by $\mathrm{~8~mL}$ $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ -saturated Milli-Q water in a $50\mathrm{-mL}$ round-bottom flask. Then $1~\mathrm{mL}$ (3-aminopropyl) trimethoxysilane was added into the flask. The above solution was stirred for $10~\mathrm{min}$ . The mixed solution was then transferred into a $30\mathrm{-mL}$ exclusive vitreous vessel. After 120-min microwave reaction at $160^{\circ}\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{i}}$ , the sample was collected. Dialysis $(500~\mathrm{Da})$ was used to purify the obtained sample. The final products were obtained as a white powder by freeze-drying.
## 4.2 Synthesis of MOFs
A type of Zn-based MOFs (namely $[\mathrm{Zn(TPA)(DMF)}])$ featuring room-temperature phosphorescence was synthesized according to a previously reported method [35]. Briefly, 1,4-dicarboxybenzene (TPA, $\mathrm{166~mg}$ ) and $10~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF were added to the exclusive vitreous vessel and sonicated for $20\mathrm{min}$ . Then, $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O}$ $(595~\mathrm{mg})$ and water $(0.5~\mathrm{mL})$ ) were added to the vessel. After 6-h reaction at $100\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the sample was collected when the temperature cooled to $<30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ naturally. Finally, the obtained sample was washed with DMF several times.
## 4.3 Synthesis of MOFs@SiNPs
In details, TPA $\mathrm{166~mg)}$ and DMF ( $\mathrm{.10~mL}$ ) were added to the vitreous vessel and sonicated for $20\mathrm{min}$ . Then, $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O}$ $(595~\mathrm{mg})$ ), SiNPs $(0.0020\ \mathrm{g})$ and water $(0.5~\mathrm{mL}$ ) were added to the the vessel. After 6-h reaction at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the sample was collected when the temperature cooled to $<30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ naturally. The obtained precipitate was washed with DMF.
## 4.4 Anti-counterfeiting application
The desired numbers were printed onto papers and then these numbers were drawn with the corresponding materials (i.e., $\mathrm{MOFs@SiNPs,:}$ SiNPs and $\mathsf{M O F s@S i N P s}$ dispersions in NaOH solution $\mathrm{\langlepH}=13)$ ). The fluorescence and phosphorescence images were captured by a digital camera under $365\ \mathrm{nm}$ irradiation and UV excitation off, respectively. Typically, a Maestro in vivo optical imaging system (Cambridge Research & instrumentation, Inc.) was utilized for imaging the fingerprints on commercial black paper developed with the $\mathsf{M O F s@S i N P s}$ ink. In particular, excitation wavelength: $365~\mathrm{nm}$ (an additional UV lamp), blue emission window: $420{\mathrm{-}}460\ \mathrm{nm}$ and orange emission window: $580{-}620~\mathrm{nm}$ , exposure time: $1{,}000\ \mathrm{ms};$ ; UV excitation off, green emission window: $490{-}530~\mathrm{nm}$ , exposure time: $1,000\mathrm{ms}$ .
